+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   If $\sigma$ is too large, points may fall far from the surface, effectively becoming uniform volume samples and losing the benefit of near-surface density [S1].
+*   If $\sigma$ is too small, the points may be numerically indistinguishable from the surface, providing little gradient information for the SDF [S1].
+*   The method assumes the surface samples are accurate; noise will propagate surface errors into the volume [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Distance Field Foot Point Generation](6946-distance-field-foot-point-generation.md)
-Extracts surface points from a clamped SDF volume by projecting voxels along the gradient. Chosen when working directly with volume data rather than mesh samples.
+Projects grid voxels onto the isosurface along the gradient direction. Chose when working with clamped signed distance field volumes (DFV) and needing single-step projection without iterative refinement.
 
 ### [Primary Edge Sampling](7168-primary-edge-sampling.md)
-Samples points from the intersection of the implicit surface and orientation function. Chosen when view-dependent sampling is required.
+Samples points on the primary boundary curve (silhouettes) of implicit surfaces. Chose when targeting visibility discontinuities for differentiable rendering.
+
+### [Surface and Volume Training Position Sampling](9996-surface-and-volume-training-position-sampling.md)
+Mixes uniform volume, uniform surface, and perturbed surface samples. Chose when needing balanced coverage of both the object interior/exterior and the immediate surface vicinity for neural implicit reconstruction.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of surface samples. Each sample requires a constant number of random number generations and additions.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the perturbed samples.
-- **Performance:** Very fast, suitable for real-time resampling during training [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, effectively increases sample density near the surface without complex geometric calculations.
-- **Limitations:** Does not guarantee uniform distribution near the surface; quality depends on the initial surface sample distribution.
-- **When to use:** When training implicit surface representations and surface samples are already available.
-- **When not to use:** When working directly with volume data or when view-dependent sampling is required.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of surface samples. Each point requires a constant number of random number generations and additions.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the resulting points.
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; effectively increases the density of training points near the surface, which is crucial for SDF regression [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Does not guarantee coverage of the entire volume; relies on the quality of the initial surface samples [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Training neural implicit surfaces where near-surface gradients are critical [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When uniform volumetric coverage is required without surface bias, or when surface samples are unavailable.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of Neural Geometric Level of Detail (NGLOD) for real-time rendering with implicit 3D shapes [S1]. The method is part of a training pipeline that resamples points at every epoch to maintain a balanced distribution of surface, near-surface, and uniform samples [S1].
+The method was introduced in the context of Neural Geometric Level of Detail (NGLOD) for real-time rendering with implicit 3D shapes [S1]. It is part of a training strategy that distributes points in a 2:2:1 split of surface, near, and uniform samples to optimize the implicit representation [S1]. The approach leverages the fact that perturbing surface points provides valuable gradient information for learning the signed distance field near the zero-level set [S1].
 
